    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The declaration set out a single set of rights held to be universal, valid at all times and in every place, and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to reside in the Nation rather than in a king.</li><li>Its rights belonged only to men, and the 1791 Constitution divided men into active and passive citizens, so that only about 4.3 million of some 29 million people were granted the vote.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympe-de-gouges">Olympe de Gouges</a> answered it with a declaration for women, and it inspired the uprisings of enslaved people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, though it did not mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympe-de-gouges">Olympe de Gouges</a>'s Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen of 1791 follows the seventeen articles of the 1789 text point for point, and Camille Naish has described it as &quot;almost a parody&quot; of the original.</p></aside>
    </section>

